---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Download"
  description="OpenWebHMI is pre-alpha. Build from source today; pre-built installers for the gateway and designer ship in Phase 4."
>
  <div class="container download">
    <header class="download-header">
      <span class="badge warn">Pre-alpha · no installers yet</span>
      <h1>Download</h1>
      <p class="lede muted">
        Pre-built installers for the gateway and the Tauri designer ship in
        Phase 4 (1.0 release). Today, you build from source — the workflow
        below works on macOS, Linux, and Windows.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="download-section">
      <h2>Prerequisites</h2>
      <ul class="prereqs">
        <li>
          <strong>Rust</strong> — stable toolchain (pinned in
          <code>rust-toolchain.toml</code>).
          <a href="https://rustup.rs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Install via rustup ↗</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Node 20+</strong> and <strong>pnpm 9+</strong>.
          <a href="https://pnpm.io/installation" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">pnpm install instructions ↗</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Python 3.11+</strong> (Phase 3 onward; not needed for Phase 1).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Tauri prerequisites</strong> for the designer (Phase 2).
          <a href="https://tauri.app/start/prerequisites/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Tauri docs ↗</a>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="download-section">
      <h2>Build from source</h2>
      <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/sergiogallegos/OpenWebHMI.git
cd OpenWebHMI
pnpm install
cargo build --workspace</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="download-section">
      <h2>Run the Phase 1 demo</h2>
      <p class="muted">
        Three terminals. After this, the runtime page shows a sin wave, a
        counter, and a Pressure value coming from the Rockwell-protocol
        simulator — all updating live. Killing the simulator flips the value
        to a Bad-quality state; restarting it recovers automatically.
      </p>

      <h3>1 · Start the EtherNet/IP simulator</h3>
      <pre><code>cargo run -p sim-rockwell -- --bind 127.0.0.1 --port 44818</code></pre>

      <h3>2 · Start the gateway with the demo project</h3>
      <pre><code>cargo run -p openwebhmi-gateway -- \
  --project examples/projects/phase1-demo/project.toml</code></pre>

      <h3>3 · Open the web runtime</h3>
      <pre><code>pnpm --filter @openwebhmi/runtime-web dev
# Then open http://localhost:5173</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="download-section">
      <h2>Coming in Phase 4 (1.0)</h2>
      <ul class="future">
        <li>
          <strong>Gateway installers</strong>: macOS <code>.pkg</code>,
          Linux <code>.deb</code>/<code>.rpm</code>, Windows MSI, and a
          Docker image.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Designer installers</strong>: Tauri-bundled
          <code>.dmg</code> for macOS and signed installer for Windows.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Single-line installers</strong> via Homebrew, Scoop, and a
          curl-pipe-shell script.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Hosted demo gateway</strong> at
          <code>demo.openwebhmi.dev</code> for evaluating the runtime
          without local setup.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="download-section warning">
      <h2>Status reminder</h2>
      <p>
        OpenWebHMI is <strong>pre-alpha</strong>. It is not safety-rated, not
        a hard-real-time control platform, and not intended for production
        deployment in its current state. Real-hardware validation against a
        physical Rockwell PLC is a hard gate before tagging 1.0.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .download {
    padding: 56px 0;
  }

  .download-header {
    text-align: center;
    margin-bottom: 48px;
  }

  .download-header h1 {
    margin: 16px 0 12px;
  }

  .download-section {
    margin-top: 40px;
  }

  .prereqs,
  .future {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 16px 0 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 10px;
  }

  .prereqs li,
  .future li {
    padding: 14px 18px;
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
  }

  .download-section.warning {
    margin-top: 56px;
    background: rgba(245, 158, 11, 0.08);
    border: 1px solid rgba(245, 158, 11, 0.3);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    padding: 24px;
  }

  .download-section.warning h2 {
    margin-top: 0;
    color: var(--warn);
  }
</style>
